body:
I don't think the memory leak is from the `byte[]` array, since you are reusing it. The memory consumption is likely increasing because of the `Image` and `ImageTexture` allocations every 0.1 seconds. If I had to guess why the `System.Array.Clone` "fixes" it, it's likely that it's adding enough pressure to the GC to force a collection, which will clean up all the `Image` and `ImageTexture` references no longer referenced by anything.

You can see this by calling `GC.Collect()` manually, this means it's not a memory leak. It's just that the GC hasn't collected your unreferenced instances yet. It's usually good practice to [dispose](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.idisposable.dispose) objects when you no longer need them.

A better alternative to creating new `Image` and `ImageTexture` instances every 0.1 seconds would be to use the `SetData` and `Update` APIs, like this:

```csharp

    public override void _Ready()
    {
        var size = _TexRect.Size;
        Data = new byte[((int)size.X * (int)size.Y * 4)];
        var w = (int)size.X;
        var h = (int)size.Y;
        _Image = Image.CreateFromData(w, h, false, Image.Format.Rgba8, Data);
        _TexRect.Texture = ImageTexture.CreateFromImage(_Image);
    }

    public void OnTimeout()
    {
        var size = _TexRect.Size;
        var w = (int)size.X;
        var h = (int)size.Y;

        for (int i = 0; i < Data.Length; i++)
        {
            Data[i] = (byte)(GD.Randi() % 256);
        }

        _Image.SetData(w, h, false, Image.Format.Rgba8, Data);
        ((ImageTexture)_TexRect.Texture).Update(_Image);
    }
```
